from qb import Q, O

Q("P1-Q32", "1.3",
  "In the research system, the coordinator launches the synthesis subagent with the instruction 'Synthesize the research findings into a draft.' The subagent returns a generic draft with no specific facts and no citations, although the search and analysis subagents produced rich results minutes earlier. What is the most likely fix?",
  [O("Include the complete findings from the search and analysis subagents directly in the synthesis subagent's prompt, with content separated from source metadata.", True,
     "Correct. Subagents do not inherit context, so everything they need must be passed explicitly."),
   O("Rely on the synthesis subagent to read the coordinator's conversation history, which it inherits when the Task tool spawns it.", False,
     "Subagents operate with isolated context and do not inherit the coordinator's history."),
   O("Add a shared memory store that every subagent reads automatically at startup, then tell the coordinator to stop passing results.", False,
     "Subagents do not share memory between invocations by default, and removing explicit context passing makes the problem worse."),
   O("Have the synthesis subagent repeat the web searches itself so it has its own fresh evidence to summarize.", False,
     "This duplicates work, widens the subagent's role, and gives it tools outside its specialization.")],
  "Subagents start with isolated context. The coordinator must provide complete findings from prior agents directly in the subagent's prompt, ideally in a structured format that separates content from metadata such as source URLs and page numbers so attribution survives.",
  "Explicit context passing to subagents (Task 1.3).",
  "A generic draft with no specifics is what a subagent produces when it has been given nothing but an instruction.",
  "Never assume implicit context sharing between agents. Pass what is needed.")

Q("P1-Q33", "1.2",
  "The web search subagent discovers a PDF URL and, to save time, calls the document analysis subagent directly. In production, a failure in the document analysis step is invisible to the coordinator, and nobody can reconstruct which documents were analyzed. What architecture change is best?",
  [O("Route all inter-subagent communication through the coordinator, which receives the URL, decides whether to invoke document analysis, and handles any errors.", True,
     "Correct. Hub-and-spoke provides observability, consistent error handling, and controlled information flow."),
   O("Keep the direct calls and have each subagent write a log line describing the call to a shared file.", False,
     "Logs improve visibility but the coordinator still cannot manage errors, and responsibility becomes distributed."),
   O("Merge the search and document analysis subagents into one agent so no inter-agent calls are required.", False,
     "This removes specialization and tool scoping, and the coordinator would still lack visibility into steps."),
   O("Let the document analysis subagent call back the search subagent whenever it needs more context, forming a peer loop.", False,
     "Peer-to-peer chains make flow harder to trace and control, which is the original problem.")],
  "A hub-and-spoke design has the coordinator manage all inter-subagent communication, error handling, and information routing. This gives observability and consistent recovery and prevents hidden failures.",
  "Hub-and-spoke orchestration (Task 1.2).",
  "Invisible failure and untraceable processing are consequences of bypassing the coordinator.",
  "Centralize routing so you have one place to observe, log, and recover.")

Q("P1-Q34", "1.2",
  "The research coordinator always runs web search, document analysis, synthesis, and report generation for every query. Simple factual questions such as 'In what year was the company founded?' take as long and cost as much as broad research requests. What should change?",
  [O("Have the coordinator analyze each query's complexity and dynamically choose which subagents to invoke, skipping stages that are not needed.", True,
     "Correct. Adaptive selection avoids unnecessary work for simple queries."),
   O("Add a keyword filter before the coordinator that sends any question beginning with 'what' or 'when' straight to a cached answer table.", False,
     "Keyword matching misroutes many questions and requires a table that cannot cover arbitrary facts."),
   O("Keep the full pipeline but run it on a smaller, faster model for every query to reduce cost.", False,
     "This lowers cost per run but still performs unneeded stages and may degrade complex research."),
   O("Run every stage in parallel for all queries so that total time equals the slowest single stage.", False,
     "Later stages depend on earlier outputs, and parallelizing does not remove the unnecessary work.")],
  "Coordinators should analyze query requirements and dynamically select which subagents to invoke rather than always routing through the full pipeline. More agents are not always better.",
  "Dynamic subagent selection (Task 1.2).",
  "Identical cost for simple and broad queries is the sign that the coordinator does not adapt.",
  "Match orchestration effort to task complexity.")

Q("P1-Q35", "1.3",
  "A developer defines AgentDefinitions for search, analysis, synthesis, and report subagents. When the coordinator runs, it tries to answer research queries by itself and never delegates. Logs show no subagent invocations. The coordinator's allowedTools list contains Read and WebSearch. What should be changed?",
  [O("Add \"Task\" (the subagent-spawning tool) to the coordinator's allowedTools so it can spawn subagents.", True,
     "Correct. The Task tool is the mechanism for spawning subagents, and it must be allowed."),
   O("Add a sentence to the system prompt saying 'You must delegate everything'.", False,
     "Without the Task tool in allowedTools, the coordinator cannot delegate no matter how it is instructed."),
   O("Rename the subagents so their names better describe their roles, then re-run.", False,
     "Names do not enable invocation."),
   O("Give each subagent the full set of tools so they can be reached without a coordinator.", False,
     "This does not create a way for the coordinator to spawn them and breaks tool scoping.")],
  "The Task tool is how subagents are spawned, and allowedTools must include \"Task\" for a coordinator to invoke them. Without it, the coordinator can only use its own tools.",
  "Task tool and allowedTools configuration (Task 1.3).",
  "No subagent invocations in the logs plus an allowedTools list without Task is decisive.",
  "When delegation never occurs, check capability before prompting.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q36", "1.1",
  "A search subagent's loop ends whenever the assistant's message includes the text 'FINAL ANSWER'. Sometimes the model forgets the phrase and the loop runs until a safety timeout, and sometimes it writes the phrase while still requesting a tool. What is the correct termination logic?",
  [O("End the loop when the response's stop_reason is 'end_turn', and keep executing requested tools while it is 'tool_use'.", True,
     "Correct. The API's stop_reason is the reliable control signal."),
   O("Keep the phrase check but add a second phrase such as 'DONE' so either one ends the loop.", False,
     "More phrases are still natural-language parsing and still fail when the model omits or misuses them."),
   O("End the loop after three iterations in all cases so it cannot run for long.", False,
     "An arbitrary cap truncates legitimate multi-step searches and does not reflect task completion."),
   O("End the loop as soon as the assistant message contains any text content.", False,
     "Text can accompany tool_use blocks, so this would end the loop prematurely.")],
  "Loops should continue on stop_reason 'tool_use' and terminate on 'end_turn'. Parsing natural language, relying on arbitrary iteration caps as the primary mechanism, or checking for text content are all anti-patterns.",
  "Loop termination by stop_reason (Task 1.1).",
  "Both failure modes (missing phrase, phrase plus tool call) arise from using text as the signal.",
  "Use structured signals for control flow, never prose.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q37", "1.7",
  "A researcher uses a named session to investigate a market over several days with --resume. Overnight, two of the source documents the agent analyzed were revised. The researcher wants the investigation to continue accurately without re-reading everything. What is the best approach?",
  [O("Resume the named session and tell the agent which two documents changed, so it re-analyzes those specific files and updates the affected conclusions.", True,
     "Correct. Prior context is mostly valid, so targeted re-analysis is efficient."),
   O("Resume the session without mentioning the change, since the agent will notice changes in its tools automatically.", False,
     "The agent has no way to know the documents changed unless it is told or re-reads them."),
   O("Delete the session and re-run the whole investigation from the beginning to be safe.", False,
     "This wastes work when only two documents changed."),
   O("Fork the session and let the forked branch assume the original conclusions remain correct.", False,
     "A fork keeps the same stale conclusions and offers no benefit here.")],
  "When resuming after changes, inform the agent about specific changes so it can do targeted re-analysis rather than full re-exploration. Resumption is appropriate when prior context is mostly valid; fresh starts with summaries are better when most results are stale.",
  "Resuming sessions after changes (Task 1.7).",
  "Only two documents changed, so most of the history remains valid and a targeted update is the efficient choice.",
  "Tell resumed agents what changed instead of making them rediscover it.")

Q("P1-Q38", "1.3",
  "The coordinator currently spawns the web search subagent, waits, then spawns the document analysis subagent in the next turn, even though the two tasks are independent. Its prompt also lists numbered steps ('1. search for X, 2. open each result...') that sometimes block the subagents from adapting to unexpected sources. Which redesign addresses both issues?",
  [O("Emit multiple Task calls in a single coordinator response for the independent subagents, and rewrite the coordinator's guidance to state research goals and quality criteria instead of procedural steps.", True,
     "Correct. Parallel Task calls reduce latency and goal-based prompts allow subagents to adapt."),
   O("Run both subagents from application code in two threads and keep the numbered steps so behavior stays predictable.", False,
     "Moving orchestration out of the model removes coordinator judgment and keeps the rigid steps."),
   O("Have the web search subagent spawn the document analysis subagent as soon as it finds a file, and shorten the prompt.", False,
     "Subagent-to-subagent spawning bypasses the coordinator and fails to address the procedural prompt."),
   O("Replace both subagents with one agent that performs search and analysis in sequence from a long list of instructions.", False,
     "This loses specialization and parallelism and makes the prompt more procedural.")],
  "Parallel subagents are spawned by emitting multiple Task tool calls in one coordinator response. Coordinator prompts should specify goals and quality criteria rather than step-by-step procedures so subagents can adapt.",
  "Parallel spawning and goal-oriented prompts (Task 1.3).",
  "Independent tasks across separate turns and rigid numbered steps each map to a specific correction.",
  "Parallelize independent work and describe outcomes, not procedures.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q39", "2.2",
  "The web search subagent's tool wrapper catches network timeouts and returns an empty results list marked successful. In the final report, the synthesis says 'no evidence was found that vendor X had security incidents'. Later it turns out the search never ran. What change best prevents this?",
  [O("Distinguish access failures from valid empty results: return a structured error (failure type, attempted query, partial results) for timeouts, and a success with zero matches only when the search actually ran.", True,
     "Correct. The coordinator can retry, rephrase, or annotate the gap."),
   O("Treat every empty result as a failure and retry it three times before giving up.", False,
     "Genuine searches may legitimately return nothing, and retrying them wastes effort."),
   O("Keep empty-success responses but add a low-confidence label to the final report in every case.", False,
     "A blanket label does not tell the reader or coordinator which searches actually failed."),
   O("Have the coordinator re-run every empty search with broader terms to see if something appears.", False,
     "This does not tell the coordinator whether the original query executed, and may broaden irrelevantly.")],
  "Access failures (timeouts needing retry decisions) and valid empty results (successful queries with no matches) mean different things. Silently converting failures to empty successes suppresses errors and leads to false claims of absence.",
  "Access failure versus valid empty result (Task 2.2, 5.3).",
  "The report asserted absence of evidence from a search that never happened, which is the damage caused by hiding the failure.",
  "Never let an error masquerade as an empty answer.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q40", "2.4",
  "The document analysis subagent spends many turns calling list and search tools just to find out which internal reports exist before it can analyze anything. The team has a reliable index of all reports with titles, dates, and topics. How can you reduce these exploratory calls?",
  [O("Expose the report catalog as an MCP resource so the agent can see what is available without exploratory tool calls.", True,
     "Correct. MCP resources are designed to expose content catalogs."),
   O("Add another search tool with a similar name so the agent has more ways to find reports.", False,
     "More overlapping tools add selection ambiguity and do not give an overview."),
   O("Embed the full text of all reports in the system prompt so the agent already has everything.", False,
     "That consumes massive context and does not scale."),
   O("Tell the agent to guess likely report titles and open them directly.", False,
     "Guessing wastes calls and misses reports.")],
  "MCP resources expose content catalogs such as documentation hierarchies, issue summaries, or database schemas so agents have visibility into available data without exploratory tool calls.",
  "MCP resources for catalogs (Task 2.4).",
  "The agent is spending turns discovering what exists, which is what a catalog resource answers up front.",
  "Use resources for 'what is available' and tools for 'do something'.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q41", "2.1",
  "The coordinator frequently sends PDF analysis requests to analyze_content and web page requests to analyze_document, although both tools have nearly identical descriptions ('Analyzes content and returns findings'). Which fix is most effective?",
  [O("Rename the tools to purpose-specific names (for example extract_web_results and analyze_document_pdf) and rewrite descriptions with inputs, outputs, and boundaries for each.", True,
     "Correct. Clear names and differentiated descriptions eliminate the overlap that causes misrouting."),
   O("Keep the names and add a system prompt line saying 'use analyze_content for the internet and analyze_document for files'.", False,
     "A prompt patch leaves ambiguous tool definitions and depends on the model following the rule."),
   O("Delete one of the tools and route all requests through the remaining one, keeping its generic description.", False,
     "Consolidating without clarifying inputs, outputs, and boundaries leaves the model's selection problem unsolved."),
   O("Force the model to try analyze_content first for every request and switch to analyze_document only on an error.", False,
     "Trial and error doubles calls and produces avoidable failures.")],
  "Ambiguous or overlapping tool descriptions cause misrouting. Renaming tools and writing descriptions that clearly differentiate purpose, inputs, outputs, and boundaries is the primary fix.",
  "Tool description overlap (Task 2.1).",
  "Identical descriptions are the cause, so the fix must create differences in definitions.",
  "If two tools look alike to you, they look alike to the model.")

Q("P1-Q42", "4.6",
  "The research system uses the same agent instance to write a report and then review it. The reviewer keeps approving the report even though a spot check finds an unsupported claim. What is the best improvement?",
  [O("Use a second, independent Claude instance without the generator's reasoning context to review the report.", True,
     "Correct. Independent review is more likely to question decisions made during generation."),
   O("Ask the generating agent to review its work again with a more critical tone.", False,
     "The same session retains its own reasoning and tends not to question its decisions."),
   O("Increase the length of the report so the reviewer has more material to evaluate.", False,
     "Longer reports make review harder, not better."),
   O("Remove the review step and rely on the quality of the initial generation.", False,
     "This removes the safeguard that caught issues.")],
  "A model retains its reasoning context from generation, making it less likely to question its own decisions. An independent instance without that context is more effective at catching subtle issues.",
  "Independent review instances (Task 4.6).",
  "Same-instance review approving flawed output is the stated risk.",
  "Review with fresh eyes, meaning a fresh context.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q43", "4.6",
  "A cited research report passes schema validation: every claim has a URL, the URLs resolve, and the formatting is perfect. A manual audit shows 11% of claims are not actually supported by the cited page. Which verification design is most effective?",
  [O("Run a separate verifier instance that receives only each claim and its cited excerpt, without the generator's reasoning, and returns supported or unsupported with the evidence.", True,
     "Correct. Independent, focused verification tests semantic support."),
   O("Ask the writing agent to re-check its own citations using extended thinking before finalizing.", False,
     "The same instance retains its generation context and is less likely to challenge itself."),
   O("Add a stricter schema rule that every claim must include at least two URLs.", False,
     "More URLs make the output look better but say nothing about whether the sources support the claim."),
   O("Run the generation three times and keep only claims that appear in at least two runs.", False,
     "Agreement among runs of the same model can repeat the same error and may suppress true but intermittent claims.")],
  "Syntactically valid citations do not guarantee semantic support. Independent review instances without the generator's reasoning context are more effective than self-review or extended thinking at catching subtle issues.",
  "Independent verification and semantic versus syntactic validity (Task 4.6, 5.6).",
  "The structural checks already pass, so the missing control must examine meaning, from an independent viewpoint.",
  "Schema validity is necessary, never sufficient.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q44", "4.6",
  "A single-pass review of 12 analyzed source documents produces thorough comments on the first few, shallow comments on the rest, and contradictory judgments about the same statistic in two documents. What restructuring is best?",
  [O("Run a focused per-document pass for local issues, then a separate integration pass that compares claims across documents.", True,
     "Correct. This avoids attention dilution and handles cross-document consistency explicitly."),
   O("Only accept findings that appear in at least two of three full-length single-pass runs.", False,
     "Consensus filtering can discard real issues found inconsistently and does not fix the depth problem."),
   O("Ask the reviewer to be consistent across all documents and to treat all documents with equal care.", False,
     "Instruction alone does not change attention dilution."),
   O("Split the batch into two sets of six documents and review each set in a separate session without any later comparison.", False,
     "Smaller sets help depth but remove cross-document comparison, so contradictions persist.")],
  "Large multi-item reviews suffer attention dilution. Splitting into per-item local analysis plus a dedicated cross-item integration pass yields consistent depth and handles cross-document issues.",
  "Multi-pass review architecture (Task 4.6, 1.6).",
  "Uneven depth and contradictory judgments are the signature of attention dilution in one pass.",
  "Local passes for depth, integration passes for relationships.")

Q("P1-Q45", "4.2",
  "The document analysis subagent extracts the methodology of each paper. It returns null for methodology on papers where the method is described in narrative paragraphs rather than a labelled 'Methods' section. Which prompt change is likely most effective?",
  [O("Add few-shot examples showing correct extraction from documents with different structures, including one where methodology is embedded in narrative text.", True,
     "Correct. Examples of varied structures address the empty extraction caused by format differences."),
   O("Mark methodology as required in the schema so the model must always produce a value.", False,
     "Forcing a required field encourages fabrication when the information is hard to find rather than teaching where to look."),
   O("Instruct the model to search harder and to avoid returning null whenever possible.", False,
     "A general instruction does not teach recognition of narrative methodology."),
   O("Pre-process every paper to detect the 'Methods' heading and skip papers without one.", False,
     "This excludes valid papers and does not solve the extraction problem.")],
  "Few-shot examples demonstrating extraction from varied document structures reduce empty or null extraction of fields that exist in non-standard locations.",
  "Few-shot examples for varied formats (Task 4.2).",
  "The nulls appear only for narrative-format papers, so the model needs examples of that format.",
  "When the failure correlates with format, add format-diverse examples.", diff="C")

Q("P1-Q46", "5.6",
  "In a research pipeline, the summarization step condenses ten findings into one paragraph. The final report states a statistic but nobody can tell which source it came from. What should the pipeline have done?",
  [O("Require structured claim-source mappings (claim, source URL or document, excerpt) from subagents and make the synthesis agent preserve and merge them.", True,
     "Correct. Mappings keep attribution through summarization."),
   O("Ask the summarizer to add a general line listing all sources used at the end of the paragraph.", False,
     "A source list does not connect individual claims to sources."),
   O("Remove statistics from reports to avoid unattributed numbers.", False,
     "This discards valuable content instead of preserving attribution."),
   O("Have the report generator search the web again to rediscover where each statistic came from.", False,
     "Rediscovery is unreliable and wasteful when the information existed earlier.")],
  "Source attribution is lost when findings are compressed without preserving claim-source mappings. Structured mappings must be required and carried through synthesis.",
  "Provenance preservation (Task 5.6).",
  "The statistic lost its origin at a summarization step, so the structure must travel with the claim.",
  "Treat provenance as data, not as prose.", diff="F")

Q("P1-Q47", "5.3",
  "During a research run, the web search subagent times out on one of five sub-topics after retrying locally. The rest of the pipeline succeeds. Which behavior is best for the final deliverable?",
  [O("Return structured error context (failure type, attempted query, partial results, alternatives) to the coordinator, which continues with available findings and annotates the report with the coverage gap.", True,
     "Correct. The report remains useful and is honest about what is missing."),
   O("Terminate the entire run and tell the user the research failed, since a report with a missing sub-topic would be incomplete.", False,
     "Failing everything for one sub-topic wastes valid work."),
   O("Silently omit that sub-topic and present the report as complete.", False,
     "Silent omission misleads the reader about coverage."),
   O("Retry the failing search indefinitely until it succeeds before producing any output.", False,
     "Unbounded retries can hang the system and may never succeed.")],
  "Subagents should recover locally from transient failures and propagate only unresolved errors with structured context. The coordinator can proceed with partial results and annotate coverage gaps. Silent suppression and total termination are both anti-patterns.",
  "Error propagation and coverage annotations (Task 5.3).",
  "A local retry already occurred and four of five sub-topics succeeded, so partial results with disclosure is optimal.",
  "Fail visibly and partially, not silently or catastrophically.", diff="C")
